[[:db/retract 17592186262461 :answer-field/choices 17592186261907] [:db/retract 17592186262461 :answer-field/choices 17592186261610] [:db/retract 17592186262461 :answer-field/choices 17592186260810] [:db/retract 17592186262461 :answer-field/choices 17592186260989] [:db/retract 17592186262461 :answer-field/choices 17592186261487] {:db/id 17592186287769 :question/problem "![](images/8b/8be62d80165b756aedf31e998d6636d7f77a5606e195b3ec288cb0ac6fb52d6f.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$144$\n\n$\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=4$ and vertical radius $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=144.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(0,4).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n9(0)^{2}+16(4)^{2}\\,>\\,144.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}>144.\n$$\n\nNotice that we use $>$ since the boundary is excluded from the set (the boundary curve is dashed).\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}>144\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287712 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A=\\{{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,…\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains four elements:\n\n$$\nB=\\{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=2{sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC=\\{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,2{sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,…\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186262695 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 3t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+t \\\\ -5+3t \\\\ 4-2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ And finally, the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2+t \\\\ y=-5+3t \\\\ z=4-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186262461 :answer-field/correct "answer-b6cefd44-c970-5d19-8f31-e184862531ce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-05138d70-95c1-5979-b58d-e293bc770f12" "answer-b6cefd44-c970-5d19-8f31-e184862531ce" "answer-cac3e014-58d3-578a-97e4-18352d84f826" "answer-b7014cb4-4b9b-5046-a88f-19f20b69fb75" "answer-90ed5060-8eb2-5907-9d5e-81459c8f8c02"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05138d70-95c1-5979-b58d-e293bc770f12" :answer/id #uuid "05138d70-95c1-5979-b58d-e293bc770f12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-4+3t \\\\ y=-3-2t \\\\ z=4-2t\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6cefd44-c970-5d19-8f31-e184862531ce" :answer/id #uuid "b6cefd44-c970-5d19-8f31-e184862531ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2+t \\\\ y=-5+3t \\\\ z=4-2t\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cac3e014-58d3-578a-97e4-18352d84f826" :answer/id #uuid "cac3e014-58d3-578a-97e4-18352d84f826" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3t \\\\ y=-5+2t \\\\ z=-4+2t\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7014cb4-4b9b-5046-a88f-19f20b69fb75" :answer/id #uuid "b7014cb4-4b9b-5046-a88f-19f20b69fb75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3t \\\\ y=5-2t \\\\ z=-4-2t\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90ed5060-8eb2-5907-9d5e-81459c8f8c02" :answer/id #uuid "90ed5060-8eb2-5907-9d5e-81459c8f8c02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4-2t \\\\ y=-5-2t \\\\ z=2+3t\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186363451 :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=(t+1)^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\bbox[5pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186410428 :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-5,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge 0]=1[-5\\ge 0]=0$ (since $-5\\ge 0$ is false)\n\n- $1[-3\\le x<0]=1[-3\\le -5<0]=0$ (since $-3\\le -5$ is false)\n\n- $1[x<-3]=1[-5<-3]=1$ (since $-5<-3$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $p(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(-5) & =0\\cdot 25+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2(-5)) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x=-5<-3,$ only the third term contributes to the result." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186234005 :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+cx+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.$ What is the value of the constants $b$ and $c?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287732 :question/problem "Find the missing coefficients in the conditional definition of the set $\\{2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}\\}$ given below.\n\n$\\{2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}\\}=\\{z\\in C\\,:\\,{z}^{3}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n${z}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The given set contains the roots $z\\in C$ of some cubic polynomial $p(z).$\n\nSince the cubic polynomial has roots ${z}_{1}=2,$ ${z}_{2}=3-\\text{i},$ and ${z}_{3}=3+\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(z) & =a(z-{z}_{1})(z-{z}_{2})(z-{z}_{3}) \\\\  & =(1)(z-2)(z-(3-\\text{i}))(z-(3+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(z-2)({z}^{2}-z(3+\\text{i})-z(3-\\text{i})+(3-\\text{i})(3+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(z-2)({z}^{2}-6z+10) \\\\  & ={z}^{3}-6{z}^{2}+10z-2{z}^{2}+12z-20 \\\\  & ={z}^{3}-8{z}^{2}+22z-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as\n\n$$\n\\{2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}\\}=\\{z\\in C\\,:\\,{z}^{3}-8{z}^{2}+22z-20=0\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186342080 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-6y=10 \\\\ -x+3y=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 10 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 10 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y=5 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx-3y=5.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=5+3y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=5+3y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186406922 :question/problem "Compute $P(1<X\\le 5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18}x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186410426 :question/problem "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}, & x=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}, & x=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186410420 :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<10) & =P(y\\in \\{1,4,9\\}) \\\\  & =P(Y=1)+P(Y=4)+P(Y=9) \\\\  & =0.4+0.2+0.3 \\\\  & =0.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233494 :question/worked-solution "The given points are ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(2,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (2,1) & =(\\frac{7+a}{2},\\frac{-4+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=\\frac{7+a}{2} \\\\ 1=\\frac{-4+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}a=-3 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(-3,6)$ and $a+b=3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198716 :question/problem "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=\\{1\\}\\times [0,1]=\\{(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71dd97607df48caf579c2e476520507ede035f65c336064f022cd900b6c120fc.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,i]=\\{(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/4b/4b87c830620f54e5b3a3bb7923be971b4d9d4abb98cfb3dcdc8387f376a0b452.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/6b/6b5b39546eabacdf948bd87e793727fdc8cd8f2664df037be53bfb630fc41670.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
